/**
 * AccountCountrySync
 *
 * When an account's BillingCountry changes, the contacts that "follow the company"
 * get their MailingCountry rewritten:
 *   - contacts with a blank MailingCountry, or
 *   - contacts whose MailingCountry equals the account's PREVIOUS BillingCountry.
 *
 * Contacts that already show a different MailingCountry (they work abroad) and every
 * contact of an account whose BillingCountry did not change are left untouched.
 *
 * Bulk-safe by construction:
 *   - 1 SOQL statement per transaction, executed once, outside any loop
 *   - 1 DML statement per transaction (0 if there is nothing to write)
 *   - all lookups are Map/Set lookups, never a query in a loop
 */
public without sharing class AccountCountrySync {

    /**
     * @param updatedAccounts  Trigger.new - the accounts as they are now
     * @param oldAccountMap    Trigger.oldMap - the accounts as they were before the commit
     */
    public static void handle(List<Account> updatedAccounts, Map<Id, Account> oldAccountMap) {
        if (updatedAccounts == null || updatedAccounts.isEmpty() || oldAccountMap == null) {
            return;
        }

        // ---------------------------------------------------------------------
        // 1) Work out which accounts actually changed BillingCountry, and keep
        //    the old/new value for each one in a small in-memory map.
        //    Everything else in the batch is ignored from here on.
        // ---------------------------------------------------------------------
        Set<Id> changedAccountIds = new Set<Id>();
        Map<Id, String> previousCountryByAccountId = new Map<Id, String>();
        Map<Id, String> currentCountryByAccountId = new Map<Id, String>();

        for (Account acct : updatedAccounts) {
            Account oldAccount = oldAccountMap.get(acct.Id);
            if (oldAccount == null) {
                continue;
            }

            String previousCountry = oldAccount.BillingCountry;
            String currentCountry = acct.BillingCountry;

            if (sameCountry(previousCountry, currentCountry)) {
                continue; // country did not change - leave its contacts completely alone
            }

            changedAccountIds.add(acct.Id);
            previousCountryByAccountId.put(acct.Id, previousCountry);
            currentCountryByAccountId.put(acct.Id, currentCountry);
        }

        if (changedAccountIds.isEmpty()) {
            return; // nothing to do - zero SOQL, zero DML
        }

        // ---------------------------------------------------------------------
        // 2) The one and only query in this handler. It is scoped to the accounts
        //    in this transaction, so we read rows proportional to the changed
        //    accounts instead of the whole org, and we never iterate a query.
        //    Bind variable => no injection risk.
        // ---------------------------------------------------------------------
        List<Contact> contactsToUpdate = new List<Contact>();

        for (Contact con : [
            SELECT Id, AccountId, MailingCountry
            FROM Contact
            WHERE AccountId IN :changedAccountIds
        ]) {
            String previousCountry = previousCountryByAccountId.get(con.AccountId);
            String currentCountry = currentCountryByAccountId.get(con.AccountId);

            // MailingCountry is already set to something that is not the old HQ
            // country => this person works abroad, leave them alone.
            Boolean worksAbroad = !String.isBlank(con.MailingCountry)
                && !sameCountry(con.MailingCountry, previousCountry);
            if (worksAbroad) {
                continue;
            }

            // Already holds the new value (or both are blank) => skip the DML row.
            if (sameCountry(con.MailingCountry, currentCountry)) {
                continue;
            }

            con.MailingCountry = currentCountry;
            contactsToUpdate.add(con);
        }

        // ---------------------------------------------------------------------
        // 3) Single, guarded DML at the end - never inside the loop.
        // ---------------------------------------------------------------------
        if (!contactsToUpdate.isEmpty()) {
            update contactsToUpdate;
        }
    }

    /**
     * Blank-aware, case-insensitive country comparison.
     *
     * - String.isBlank covers null, '' and whitespace-only, so an account with no
     *   BillingCountry (null) matches contacts with no MailingCountry.
     * - Two blanks are "the same" country; one blank and one value are not.
     * - Country values are compared ignoring case/whitespace because these fields
     *   are free text when State/Country Picklists is not enabled: "France" vs
     *   "france" is not a company move and must not trigger a sync.
     */
    private static Boolean sameCountry(String leftCountry, String rightCountry) {
        Boolean leftBlank = String.isBlank(leftCountry);
        Boolean rightBlank = String.isBlank(rightCountry);

        if (leftBlank || rightBlank) {
            return leftBlank && rightBlank;
        }

        return leftCountry.trim().equalsIgnoreCase(rightCountry.trim());
    }
}
```

## What I changed about your approach, and why

**1. The "load all contacts once" query (`[SELECT Id, AccountId, MailingCountry FROM Contact]`) is removed — it cannot run in your org.** Three hard platform limits break it:

- **SOQL row limit:** a single query returns at most **50,000 rows**. With ~2.3M contacts, that statement throws `QueryException: SOQL statement exceeds limit maximum of 50,000 records` on the very first execution.
- **Heap limit:** 6 MB (8 MB in most contexts). 2.3M `Contact` sObjects would exhaust heap long before the query finished.
- **CPU / transaction time:** even if it ran, you would read 2.3M rows to update a handful of contacts in a 200-account MDM batch.

**2. It is still exactly one query, and there is still no query in a loop.** The statement is now `WHERE AccountId IN :changedAccountIds`, where `changedAccountIds` is the (at most 200) accounts in `Trigger.new` whose `BillingCountry` actually changed. Rows read and rows written are now proportional to the batch, not to the org: well inside the 50,000-row SOQL read cap and the 10,000-row DML cap per transaction. Accounts whose country did not change never even reach the query.

**3. The contact map is no longer needed.** Since the query is already narrowed to the accounts in this transaction, a single pass over the result rows does the job; building a `Map<Id, List<Contact>>` over 2.3M (or even 20k) rows would only burn heap for no benefit. I kept two *small* maps instead — `previousCountryByAccountId` / `currentCountryByAccountId` — so the Apex loop does pure `Map.get()` lookups.

**4. Correctness details I hardened:**
- **Old country may be `null`** (account had no billing country). `sameCountry` treats blank↔blank as equal, so a `null → 'France'` transition syncs only contacts whose `MailingCountry` is blank, and a `France → null` transition clears only the contacts that were on `France`.
- **Blank detection** uses `String.isBlank` (null, `''`, whitespace) rather than `== null`, because text fields can be empty strings.
- **Comparison is case-insensitive and trimmed.** With State/Country Picklists off, `BillingCountry`/`MailingCountry` are free text; `"France"` vs `"france"` is a casing edit, not a company move, so it does not trigger a sync (avoids pointless data churn in an MDM feed).
- **No-op writes are skipped** (`sameCountry(mailing, current)`), so the DML row count stays at exactly the contacts that really change.

**5. Security/mode:** the handler is declared `without sharing` explicitly. This is system-mode trigger automation and the integration user's incidental record visibility must not silently narrow which contacts get updated; relying on the inherited/default sharing mode would make behaviour depend on the caller.

**Operational note:** with 200 accounts per MDM batch, keep an eye on contacts-per-account — you get 50,000 SOQL rows and 10,000 DML rows per transaction. If your average exceeds ~250 contacts per account, shrink the MDM batch size (or add a server-side `MailingCountry` filter to the query, re-verifying case handling in Apex, which remains the authoritative filter).

